## 5. What the analysis established
1. [CODE] The "current" column equals variant B on all 20 cases, and variant A2c equals B on an exhaustive grid of 384 records (HTTP status None/200/503 x error marker x finish reason None/STOP/MAX_TOKENS/SAFETY x block reason x four texts x parser exception). The options therefore reduce to three genuinely different outcomes: **B ≡ A2c** (keep), **A2a**, **A1**.
2. **A1 makes the soft allowance unreachable for adapter-made records** (K04-K07, K11, K13, K14, K15 become FAIL-S). Consequence for spec line 838: a round-1 failure caused by a provider block (SF4 + SF2) or a timeout (SF1 + SF2) would carry the type SF2 (remedy: pure implementation fix only) instead of SF4 (STOP) or SF1 (timeout/delay/retry whitelist), because SF2 outranks them in the decision vector; the SF1 whitelist and the SF4 STOP would then almost never apply. 10 tests of the current suites flip (`deferred_patches/DEFERRED_PATCHES.md`, P1b).
3. **A2a keeps a transport error soft but makes every empty-bodied block hard** (K11, K13, K14): the typical block response has no text, so only a block that returns partial text (K12) would stay soft.
4. **A2b still has a hole** (K19 and 12 grid records): a record with empty text, no error marker and no status is not "received" under A2 and would produce no flag at all (counted as UNP, a measured outcome and not a failure), whereas today it is SF2. Closing the hole by allowing a status-less record without error is exactly A2c ≡ B.
5. [CODE] The record cannot distinguish "no candidate" from "a candidate without text and without `finishReason`" (K13): the adapter stores neither a candidate count nor a presence flag, and `sf_flags` uses `finish_reason is None` with HTTP 200 and empty text as the "no candidate" signal. A candidate with finish STOP and no text is SF2 (K01); the same candidate without a `finishReason` is SF4.
6. [CODE] A 2xx response whose body is not decodable JSON is stored with status None and the exception class name (K07), so the 2xx status is lost and the call is SF1, not an empty response, in every variant except A1.
7. [CODE] "Whitespace" is the set space, TAB, LF, VT, FF, CR (`sf_flags` line 59). A body of only U+00A0 is NOT SF2 (K20); it goes to parsing and, having no `Verdict:` field, is UNP (counted in the UNP share, not a structural failure). §13.4 does not define the set.
8. Hard flags (SF3, SF5) decide identically in all variants and are not affected by C-31: a call that meets SF3 or SF5 is FAIL-S whatever else it meets (K16, K17), already guaranteed under the working decision MD-01.

## 6. Evaluation of A2 as a recommendation
[READING] A2 in the form "SF2 = a received 2xx response that is empty" is not a free-standing third rule: in its strict form (A2a) it hardens empty blocks; with the block exception (A2b) it leaves a hole for status-less records; with the hole closed (A2c) it is exactly the current precedence B written as a definition. So the real choice is between **keeping B but writing it as a definition (A2c wording)**, accepting the **harder A2a**, or the **literal A1**. If the owner wants B, the definitions below make the precedence explicit and ratifiable (instead of an undocumented code condition).

### 6.1 Exact definitions that would have to be ratified (all [READING]; none is in the protocol)
- **D1. Empty response (SF2):** the call is not SF1 and not SF4, and the stored response text contains no character outside the whitespace set of D2. (This is A2c; it is equivalent to the current code.)
- **D2. Whitespace set:** exactly space (U+0020), TAB, LF, VT, FF, CR. Any other character, including U+00A0 and other Unicode spaces, is content (the call is then parsed and may be UNP).
- **D3. Received response:** a call whose record has no error marker and no HTTP status outside 2xx. A record with no status and no error marker is treated as received for D1 (it keeps the present SF2 outcome) and is separately reported as `records_without_http_status` (MD-06 may stop on it).
- **D4. Invalid body:** a body that cannot be decoded, or whose payload structure raises in the adapter, is a transport/API error (SF1) and is never an empty response, whatever the HTTP status was (the adapter then stores status None).
- **D5. Error and metadata:** an error marker, or a status outside 2xx, makes the call SF1 even if text is present (12.36, already in force); a prompt block reason or a block finish reason makes it SF4 (12.36); neither makes an empty text a separate SF2.
- **D6. No candidate:** "no candidate returned" (SF4) is recognized by HTTP 200, no error marker, `finish_reason` absent and empty text. A candidate with empty text and no finish reason is not distinguishable and is treated as no candidate; separating them needs an adapter change (a stored candidate indicator), not made.
- **D7. Soft unit:** `f_S` counts calls, not flags (working decision MD-01 items 4-5).
- **D8. Recording:** the flags list keeps every flag detected under D1-D6; SF2 is therefore not recorded for an SF1 or SF4 call (an absence by definition, not a hidden flag). If the owner wants the fact "the body was empty" kept, a separate non-SF field (`body_empty`) can be added without changing any outcome; not implemented.

Effect on spec line 838: with D1 an empty-bodied block remains an SF4 round (STOP) and a timeout remains an SF1 round (timeout/delay/retry whitelist).

